{"name":"Cotton Club","hex":"#f3e4d3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cotton","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fibrous substance:\n A soft, fibrous, usually white substance consisting of fine hairs, especially the substance around the seeds of a plant of genus Gossypium.\n Any similar soft, fibrous, white substance of fine hairs, of any origin.","A soft, fibrous, usually white substance consisting of fine hairs, especially the substance around the seeds of a plant of genus Gossypium."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of cotton."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cotton"},{"term":"club","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy object, often a kind of stick, intended for use as a bludgeoning weapon or a plaything.\n\n An implement to hit the ball in certain ball games, such as golf.\n An item used during routines, the apparatus consisting of a set of two clubs.","An implement to hit the ball in certain ball games, such as golf."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hit with a club.","To score a victory over by a large margin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/club"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cotton Club","blocks":[{"text":"The Cotton Club was a 20th-century nightclub in New York City. It was located on 142nd Street and Lenox Avenue from 1923 to 1936, then briefly in the midtown Theater District until 1940. The club operated during the United States' era of Prohibition and Jim Crow era racial segregation. Black people initially could not patronize the Cotton Club, but the venue featured many of the most popular black entertainers of the era, including musicians Fletcher Henderson, Duke Ellington, Jimmie Lunceford, Chick Webb, Louis Armstrong, Count Basie, Fats Waller, Willie Bryant; vocalists Adelaide Hall, Ethel Waters, Cab Calloway, Bessie Smith, Lillie Delk Christian, Aida Ward, Avon Long, the Dandridge Sisters, the Will Vodery choir, The Mills Brothers, Nina Mae McKinney, Billie Holiday, Midge Williams, Lena Horne, and dancers such as Katherine Dunham, Bill Robinson, The Nicholas Brothers, Charles 'Honi' Coles, Leonard Reed, Stepin Fetchit, the Berry Brothers, The Four Step Brothers, Jeni Le Gon and Earl Snakehips Tucker."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Duke_Ellington_-_publicity.JPG/330px-Duke_Ellington_-_publicity.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton_Club"},{"title":"Cotton Club (Portland, Oregon)","blocks":[{"text":"The Cotton Club was a nightclub located in North Portland, Oregon, United States. Located at 2125 N. Vancouver Avenue, the club gained attention during the 1960s as the \"only nightclub on the West Coast with wall-to-wall soul.\" Celebrities such as Cab Calloway, Sammy Davis Jr., Cass Elliot, The Kingston Trio, Joe Louis, and Archie Moore would visit the nightclub when they were in town."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton_Club_(Portland%2C_Oregon)"},{"title":"Cotton","blocks":[{"text":"Cotton is a soft, fluffy staple fiber that grows in a boll, or protective case, around the seeds of the cotton plants of the genus Gossypium in the mallow family Malvaceae. The fiber is almost pure cellulose and may contain minor percentages of waxes, fats, pectins, and water. Under natural conditions, cotton bolls facilitate the dispersal of the seeds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/CottonPlant.JPG/330px-CottonPlant.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Vu_%28magazine%29_N%C2%B077.JPG/500px-Vu_%28magazine%29_N%C2%B077.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Vu_%28magazine%29_N%C2%B077.JPG/1280px-Vu_%28magazine%29_N%C2%B077.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Vu_%28magazine%29_N%C2%B077.JPG/500px-Vu_%28magazine%29_N%C2%B077.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Adelaide Hall, star of the Cotton Club","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVu_(magazine)_N%C2%B077.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Cab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg/500px-Cab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Cab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg/1280px-Cab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a1/Cab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg/500px-Cab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cab Calloway was another of the original Cotton Club performers.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACab_Calloway_Gottlieb.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Ethel_Waters_-_1943.jpg/500px-Ethel_Waters_-_1943.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Ethel_Waters_-_1943.jpg/500px-Ethel_Waters_-_1943.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ethel Waters starred at the Cotton Club","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEthel_Waters_-_1943.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Lena_Horne_-_1941.jpg/500px-Lena_Horne_-_1941.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Lena_Horne_-_1941.jpg/1280px-Lena_Horne_-_1941.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu